{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:38:58Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 114, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\tThe edit is in. Here's the retrospective report.\n2\t\n3\t## What happened\n4\t\n5\tRound 6 (Taiga #10, \"show open/done task counts\") ran clean: every stage \u2014 story, design, both build scopes, review, test \u2014 finished in a single attempt, and review (`approve-with-followups`) and test (`pass`) both landed with substantive, specific findings rather than rubber-stamps. This is the first round in the logs with `attempts: 1` across the board. (Note: `docs/agent-rework/round-06-retro.md` on disk is stale \u2014 it describes an earlier run of round 6 that hit a branch-contamination blocker on Taiga #7; the current `round-06-logs/` is a fresh rerun against a different ticket, and this report is about that rerun.)\n6\t\n7\t## What cost time or quality\n8\t\n9\t`build-frontend-attempt1` did complete, correct work, but spent two Write calls (`build-frontend-attempt1.log:148,154`) trying to persist `docs/implementation.md` to disk \u2014 both denied by the sandbox \u2014 then devoted a third of its final reply to a `**Blocked:**` section explaining the denial and pasting the file content for \"the next attempt\" to write instead (`build-frontend-attempt1.output:8-96`). Nothing ever needed that file: `agents/reviewer.md` and `agents/tester.md` never mention `docs/implementation.md`, neither role's log references it, and `app/pipeline_runner.py:227` / `build_prompt` (`pipeline_runner.py:89-95`) show the actual handoff mechanism is the agent's own final reply text, captured and threaded to the next stage automatically \u2014 no file read involved. `git log` confirms no round has ever committed a `docs/implementation.md`. So this was self-imposed busywork: the developer inferred a \"write a handoff doc\" convention from nothing in its own role definition, then framed a non-blocker as \"Blocked,\" which is exactly the kind of noise a downstream reader has to discount.\n10\t\n11\t## What I changed\n12\t\n13\t`agents/developer.md`, Handoff section: added that the handoff **is** the final reply (the pipeline captures it directly), explicitly told the developer not to spend a Write/Edit call on `docs/implementation.md` or similar, and not to report itself blocked if such a write is denied. Evidence: `build-frontend-attempt1.log:148,154` (the two denied writes) and `build-frontend-attempt1.output:8-96` (the resulting \"Blocked\" framing and duplicated content). This is a one-section edit, not a rewrite of the role.\n14\t\n15\t## What I deliberately did not change\n16\t\n17\t- **The \"when a tool call is denied\" guidance** added in round 5 (`agents/developer.md`, per `7c7faaf`) \u2014 it worked exactly as designed here: the developer didn't discard its analysis, it handed off the exact content. The problem wasn't how it handled the denial, it was that the write should never have been attempted. Fixing the root cause makes that fallback text mostly moot for this case without needing to touch it.\n18\t- **`agents/tester.md`'s \"don't retry identically\" guidance** \u2014 `test-attempt1` hit permission denials on a real, legitimate edit (an e2e test file) and on follow-up `git`/bash checks, didn't loop, fell back to a code-trace, and reported the one unverified criterion plainly (`test-attempt1.output:24`). This is the pipeline behaving correctly, not a defect.\n19\t- **The compound-command sandbox denials in `review-attempt1.log`** (`npx oxlint ...`, `cd ... && git ...`) \u2014 the reviewer self-corrected to single-operation syntax each time and lost only a couple of turns, no misdiagnosis resulted. This looks like a sandbox/permission-profile characteristic, not a prompt problem, and cost too little to justify a change.\n20\t- **The reviewer's `#5a646c`/loading-flash follow-up finding** \u2014 thin margin-timing issue correctly triaged as non-blocking and tied to the design doc's own documented tradeoff. Working as intended.\n21\t\n22\t