{"ts": "2026-08-17T12:39:43Z", "agent": "claude-edits", "seq": 149, "kind": "message", "text": "## What happened\n\nRound 7 (Taiga #11, \"search tasks by title\") ran clean end to end: every stage \u2014 story, design, both build scopes, review, test \u2014 finished in a single attempt, review landed `approve`, and test landed `pass` with a genuinely traced check of all 7 acceptance criteria and 6 edge cases (`review-attempt1.output`, `test-attempt1.output`). The merge (`7c1276f`) is a minimal, faithful diff matching the spec and design.\n\n## What cost time or quality\n\n`tester` (stage `test`) spent a full Write call constructing the entire test report as `docs/test-review.md`, got denied by the sandbox (`test-attempt1.log:187-189`, `\"Claude requested permissions to write to ... but you haven't granted it yet\"`), and then had to reproduce the same content in its final chat reply anyway (`test-attempt1.log:196`, `test-attempt1.output:1`: *\"I attempted to write the full report to `docs/test-review.md` but that write needs your explicit approval... so I'm presenting it here instead.\"*). Nothing downstream reads that file \u2014 `app/pipeline_runner.py`'s `parse_verdict(final)` reads the verdict straight from the final reply text, the same mechanism already documented for the developer role. This is the identical self-imposed-file-write pattern round 6's retrospective fixed in `agents/developer.md` (`8538582`), just showing up in `agents/tester.md` instead, which had no equivalent guidance. Round 6 explicitly checked and found no evidence of this in the tester at the time (`round-06-retro.md`: *\"tester.md's 'don't retry identically' guidance ... this is the pipeline behaving correctly\"*), so this is new evidence, not a repeat of an already-declined fix.\n\n## What I changed\n\n`agents/tester.md`, added a paragraph after the \"Verdict\" section: the tester's report **is** its final reply, there is no `docs/test-review.md` for anything downstream to read, don't spend a Write call persisting it to disk, and don't report itself blocked if such a write is denied. Wording mirrors the fix already applied to `agents/developer.md`'s Handoff section in round 6, since it's the same root cause on a different role. Evidence: `test-attempt1.log:187-189` (the denied write) and `test-attempt1.output:1` (the resulting duplicated-effort framing).\n\n## What I deliberately did not change\n\n- **`agents/reviewer.md`** \u2014 no evidence of the same file-write pattern this round; `review-attempt1.output` went straight to findings + `VERDICT: approve` with no Write attempt. Nothing to fix.\n- **The backend developer's no-op dispatch** (`build-backend-attempt1.output`: *\"Work found in scope: none\"*) \u2014 the spec and design explicitly state zero backend changes, and the developer correctly recognized that, verified it by reading both docs, touched nothing, and reported cleanly in a few lines. This is the pipeline working as intended (parallel frontend/backend dispatch with correct scope discipline), not wasted effort worth changing an instruction over \u2014 and whether to dispatch backend at all is a machinery/pipeline.py decision, outside this role's remit anyway.\n- **Design and story stages** \u2014 both single-attempt, both specific and well-evidenced (traceability tables, contrast ratios, precedence tables). No defect to trace to an instruction gap.\n\n## Needs a human\n\nNone. The one real defect (wasted Write call + duplicated effort) was fixable entirely within `agents/tester.md`; nothing here points at `app/pipeline.py` or `app/pipeline_runner.py`.\n\nVERDICT: done", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "056c1922-269a-4d1a-896f-122a636f69fb"}}
